    'devops-docker_k8s-s2-set1' => [
        [
            'text' => 'Deep dive into Container Networking Interface (CNI): How do Calico, Cilium (eBPF), and Flannel route pod-to-pod network packets across nodes? Explain Overlay networks (VXLAN/Geneve) versus Direct Routing (BGP).',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'Overlay (VXLAN): encapsulates pod packet inside host UDP packet (adds encapsulation overhead). Direct Routing (BGP): nodes advertise pod IP CIDR blocks to physical network switches without encapsulation. Cilium uses eBPF inside Linux kernel, bypassing iptables/conntrack for ultra-fast socket-level routing.',
        ],
        [
            'text' => 'Explain Kubernetes Quality of Service (QoS) classes: Guaranteed, Burstable, and BestEffort. How does the Linux kernel OOM killer calculate `oom_score_adj` based on QoS when node memory is exhausted?',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'Guaranteed: limits == requests for all containers. Burstable: requests < limits. BestEffort: zero requests or limits. When node runs out of RAM, kernel kills BestEffort pods first (`oom_score_adj: 1000`), then Burstable pods exceeding requests, preserving Guaranteed pods (`oom_score_adj: -997`).',
        ],
        [
            'text' => 'How does etcd maintain distributed consistency in Kubernetes? Explain Raft consensus algorithm, Leader election, Write-Ahead Logging (WAL), MVCC b-tree storage, and compaction.',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'etcd uses Raft: odd number of members (3 or 5). Leader accepts writes, replicates to quorum (majority) before committing. Uses Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) where mutations create new revisions rather than in-place updates. Periodic compaction purges historical revisions to reclaim disk space.',
        ],
        [
            'text' => 'Explain Kubernetes Node Scheduling internals: Node Taints and Tolerations, Node Affinity / Anti-Affinity, and Pod Topology Spread Constraints across availability zones.',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'Taints repel pods; only pods with matching Tolerations can be scheduled on tainted nodes (e.g. dedicated GPU nodes). Node Affinity directs pods to nodes with specific labels. Topology Spread Constraints distribute replicas evenly across Availability Zones (`topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone`) to prevent single-AZ outage.',
        ],
        [
            'text' => 'How do Kubernetes NetworkPolicies enforce ingress and egress traffic filtering? What is the default-deny policy, and why do NetworkPolicies require a compatible CNI plugin (e.g. Calico / Cilium)?',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'By default, all pods in cluster can communicate freely. NetworkPolicy defines ingress/egress CIDR, namespace, and podSelector rules. Default-deny policy isolates pods. Kube-proxy does not enforce NetworkPolicies; CNI daemon (Calico/Cilium) translates policies into iptables, IP sets, or eBPF programs on host.',
        ],
